course: dictionary
lesson: 7466
title: A word for the front of a house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A porch is a small covered space at the entrance to a house, outside the front door. Read the context: «So Jamie said sorry to Mrs Reid at her door, painted her porch on Saturdays» tells you a porch is a part of the house that can be painted.
explain: Clues help you know more. The sentence about the porch light says visitors can see the porch at night, so a porch is somewhere people stand or wait by the door. Parcels can be left there too, so it is a place that is safe from rain.
explain: In some countries, such as the US and Canada, a porch can be a bigger covered area along the front or side of a house where people sit, often called a veranda. Context tells you which meaning fits: a porch near a front door, or a place to sit in the summer.
explain: Always test your meaning. Swap in «covered space outside the front door» for porch in «Her heart raced, her dog hid under the bed for a whole day, and the porch boards were scorched.» If it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: A porch is a small covered area or room at the entrance to a house, outside the front door.
fact: In the US and Canada, a porch can also be a covered area along the front or side of a house where people sit.
fact: A «porch light» is the light by the front door that helps visitors see at night.
fact: «Porch pirates» are people who steal parcels that have been left on a porch.
fact: Phrases to know: «front porch», «back porch», «porch light», «on the porch».
exercise: «So Jamie painted Mrs Reid's porch on Saturdays.» Where did Jamie paint? | In the kitchen | *The covered space outside her front door | Under the bed | On the school field
exercise: «A girl knocks only where the porch light is on.» What is a porch light? | A light for the car | A candle in the garden | *A light by the front door that helps visitors see at night | A lamp inside the bedroom
exercise: In the US, people often sit on the porch on summer evenings. What might a porch be in that sentence? | *A covered area along the front of a house where people can sit | A pond in the garden | A small room for cooking | A tall tower on the roof
test: «Her dog hid under the bed, and the porch boards were scorched.» What does scorched suggest about the porch? | Washed clean and shiny | Freshly painted in white | *Burned or marked by heat | Covered in snow
test: Why might a thief come to a porch? | To plant flowers in pots | To read a book in the sun | To sing a song for the neighbours | *To steal parcels that have been left there
exam: Swap in your meaning: «Mum left the parcel on the porch.» Which meaning fits best? | A deep well for water | A grassy field | *A covered space outside the front door | A loud bell that rings at noon
